WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:04.304
NARRATOR: It's a mystery.

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:07.273
Great whites around the world
have been attacking divers

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.311
in cages, but no one knows why.

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:15.014
This is frightening.

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.950
NARRATOR: Finally,
Dr. Greg Skomal

00:00:17.016 --> 00:00:19.352
may have cracked the case.

00:00:19.419 --> 00:00:21.187
He thinks the sharks
are chasing bait.

00:00:24.157 --> 00:00:28.428
They want to tease these
sharks in tight to the cages,

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:33.566
but that shark is going
to be disoriented.

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:36.002
NARRATOR: But there's a
problem with Greg's theory.

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:39.105
One case doesn't add up.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.106
What's going on here?

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.575
It's clearly ignoring
that first bait,

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:46.346
and that first bait's
actually pulled away from him.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.349
NARRATOR: This great white
seems to ignore the bait.

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:51.951
It goes straight for the divers.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:54.988
[music playing]

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.292
You know, you've
got to understand.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:00.493
There's a lot of
scent in the water,

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.961
so this shark is
moving right around.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.931
Let me slow things down here.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:06.332
NARRATOR: But look again.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:07.534
Another clue.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.469
GREG SKOMAL (VOICEOVER):
The shark goes to the back

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.671
of the cages, and there you go.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:12.906
Right there.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:15.775
There's a bait box
behind the cage,

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:18.711
and the shark is checking
out the bait box.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:23.249
NARRATOR: This highlighted
bait box is full of chum.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:26.453
It sends a powerful
scent to the shark.

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:29.522
But it's confused, it
can't see the fish.

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:31.391
GREG SKOMAL (VOICEOVER):
What's happened here

00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:33.460
is, as it accelerates
toward that box,

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:36.162
it realizes there's
no room for it.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.964
NARRATOR: The shark
turns, but it's

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:40.533
too late to avoid the cage.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:41.901
These are big animals.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.070
Yeah, they're fish that are
incredibly agile in the water,

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:45.872
but not when they're this size.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:48.508
You don't want to put the
baits this close to where

00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:50.043
people are diving.

00:01:55.348 --> 00:01:58.952
NARRATOR: There's a way to test
if Greg's theory is correct--

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:01.921
by seeing how sharks
react to cage divers

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:06.392
when they are drawn in by
something other than baiting.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:09.429
[music playing]

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:19.572
NARRATOR: In Australia, Marine
Biologist Melissa Marquez

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.308
and boat operator Matt
Waller, will attempt

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:24.377
to attract sharks using music.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:32.185
This experiment is
a real opportunity

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:34.954
for us to learn something new.

00:02:35.021 --> 00:02:38.024
NARRATOR: Melissa wants to see
for herself if great whites

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:41.394
will still attack a cage
when drawn in by something

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:44.063
other than bait.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:45.031
Good to go.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:48.968
Because if there's
other stimuli

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:52.305
such as just vibrations,
and there's not food,

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:54.440
they might act a
little bit different.

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:59.212
MATT WALLER (VOICEOVER): We're
going to put her in the cage,

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:01.414
and we're going to hand
her our underwater speaker.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.429
Alright, let's start
hitting the first tune.

00:03:16.496 --> 00:03:19.399
NARRATOR: Melissa thinks
music with low frequencies

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:22.035
best peak the interest
of the sharks.

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:26.439
But will it also lead
them to ram the cage?

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:29.342
What kind of frequencies
are we talking around?

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:34.347
Forty hertz and below is
a good sweet spot, I think.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:38.051
You got anything headbangy?

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:39.185
Alright, Melissa.

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:41.020
"Darkest Hour" coming your way.

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:44.757
Get ready to rock out.

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:47.760
[rock music playing]

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:55.902
NARRATOR: A big
great white comes

00:03:55.969 --> 00:04:02.075
in not following its nose,
or its eyes, but its ears.

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:03.509
Oh yeah.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:04.477
This is more like it.

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:08.581
Yeah, I'm definitely
picking up a little bit

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:11.918
interest from the shark, here.

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:15.154
NARRATOR: Sound travels
four times faster underwater

00:04:15.221 --> 00:04:17.523
than it does through air.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.893
And Great whites
have ears to hear it.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.496
A small ear hole
sits behind each eye.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.736
There are able to home in on
the music from more than a mile

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:32.105
away.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:33.506
It makes sense
that sharks might

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:34.707
be more attracted to this.

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:39.612
After all, they love
those low frequencies.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.281
NARRATOR: The shark comes
in to check out the concert,

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:45.518
but shows no aggression at all.

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:51.257
It's evidence that no bait
means no risk of a cage attack.

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:55.094
Acoustic attraction may
offer the benefits of bait,

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:56.062
without the risks.

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:00.466
What do you think?

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:02.468
Are we crazy, or are actually
on to something maybe

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:04.337
different here?

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:06.706
We're seeing results
here, and like a lot

00:05:06.773 --> 00:05:08.441
of things with
Great White sharks,

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:10.710
more research has gotta be done.

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:14.113
[music playing]

